\u2014 yes! I have T2 and T4 \u2014 so T3 completes a chow.\n\nThat means T2 and T4 together are a waiting pair for T3.\n\nSo discarding **T4** would break this potential.\n\nSimilarly, T1-T2 can wait for T3.\n\nSo T1, T2, T4 are all involved in potential T1-T2-T3 and/or T2-T3-T4 if T3 comes.\n\nSo **T4 is NOT safe to discard** \u2014 it forms an inner chow wait with T2.\n\nSimilarly, **T1 and T2** are strong for T1-T2-T3.\n\nSo **T4** should be kept.\n\nThen what about **B5**? No such potential.\n\nOr **B2**? No.\n\nOr **F3**? Useless.\n\nSo now, the worst tile is **B5**, **B2**, **F3**, **J2**.\n\nAmong these, **F3** is a wind, but not useful.\n\nBut **J2** is dragon \u2014 slightly more valuable.\n\nSo candidates: **B5**, **B2**, **F3**\n\nB2 and B5: both Dots, but no connection.\n\nBut I have only two Dot tiles \u2014 B2 and B5 \u2014 far apart.\n\nNo chance to form chow.\n\nSo either can be discarded.\n\nBut **B5** is a five \u2014 slightly more versatile? Not really.\n\nNo pattern favors it.\n\nBut consider: **Reversible Tiles** (12 points): requires only B1,B2,B3,B4,B5,B8,B9, T2,T4,T5,T6,T8,T9, J3.\n\nI have T2, T4, T8, T9 \u2014 all reversible.\n\nB2, B5 \u2014 also reversible.\n\nJ2 \u2014 not reversible (J2 is Green Dragon, not symmetric).\n\nF3 \u2014 West Wind \u2014 not reversible.\n\nW tiles: W2,W3,W5,W6,W9 \u2014 W2,W5,W6 are reversible? According to list: only specific tiles.\n\nThe pattern says: Reversible Tiles: B1, B2, B3, B4, B5, B8, B9, T2, T4, T5, T6, T8, T9, J3.\n\nSo W tiles are not included \u2014 so Characters are not reversible.\n\nSo W2, W3, W5, W6, W9 \u2014 none are reversible.\n\nOnly:\n- T2, T4, T8, T9 \u2014 yes\n- B2, B5 \u2014 yes\n- J2 \u2014 no (only J3)\n- F3 \u2014 no\n\nSo I have T2, T4, T8, T9, B2, B5 \u2014 six reversible tiles.\n\nIf I keep only these, and discard non-reversible ones, I can aim for **Reversible Tiles** (12 points).\n\nNon-reversible tiles in hand:\n- W2, W3, W5, W6, W9 \u2014 all not reversible\n- J2 \u2014