keeping T1,T2,T8,T9,W5,W6.\n\nThen W2,W3 \u2014 marginal.\n\nNow, B2,B5,F3,J2 \u2014 must discard three of them.\n\nBut I can only keep one for pair.\n\nSo discard the rest.\n\nNow, valid actions include discarding B2, B5, F3, J2, W2, W3, etc.\n\nAmong B2, B5, F3, J2 \u2014 which to discard?\n\nJ2 and F3 are honors \u2014 but F3 is wind, not valuable.\n\nJ2 is dragon \u2014 worth 2 points if pung.\n\nSo better to keep J2 than F3.\n\nB2 and B5 \u2014 suited, could be part of chow, but no support.\n\nBut if I must keep a pair, J2 or F3 or B2/B5.\n\nBut J2 has higher scoring potential.\n\nSo keep J2 for possible dragon pair or pung.\n\nF3: no value.\n\nSo discard F3.\n\nB2 and B5: if I keep one for pair, but they are different.\n\nBut I have only one each.\n\nSo pair only if I draw another.\n\nBut odds low.\n\nBut F3 is clearly the worst: not seat wind, not prevalent wind, not dragon.\n\nDiscard F3.\n\nSimilarly, W9 is terminal, but if I use T1-T2-T3 and T7-T8-T9, then W9 is extra.\n\nBut W9 could be used in W9 + other W.\n\nBut W5,W6 are better.\n\nBut W2,W3,W5,W6,W9 \u2014 five W tiles.\n\nPossible to build multiple chows in W.\n\nFor example:\n- W2-W3-W4\n- W5-W6-W7\n- Then W9 needs W8/W7\n\nBut W7 would be shared.\n\nW5-W6-W7 and W7-W8-W9 \u2014 possible if I get W7,W8.\n\nBut I have W9.\n\nSo possible W7-W8-W9.\n\nBut I don't have W7,W8.\n\nAnd T8,T9 also want T7.\n\nConflict.\n\nBetter to commit to one suit.\n\nSince I have T1,T2 and T8,T9, and T4 (which supports T2-T3-T4 or T4-T5-T6), but T4 is less critical.\n\nBut earlier I realized T2 and T4 can form T2-T3-T4 with T3.\n\nSo T2,T4 can be used.\n\nBut T1,T2,T4 \u2014 if I get T3, I can make T1-T2-T3 and T2-T3-T4, but can't use T2 twice.\n\nSo I must choose.\n\nT1-T2-T3 is better because T1 is terminal, harder to use.\n\nT2-T3-T4 uses T2 and T4.\n\nBut T1 is only useful in T1-T2-T3.\n\nSo better to keep T1,T2 for T1-T2-T3.\n\nThen T4 is less important.\n\nBut I have both.\n\nWith T1,T2,T4, I have two chances: T1-T2-T3 or T2-T3-T4.\n\nSo T4 adds value.\n\nWhereas W2,W3 \u2014 W2-W3-W4 only.\n\nNo other use.\n\nW9 \u2014 only in W7-W8-W9 or W8-W9-W1? No, W9-W1-W2 invalid.\n\nSo W9 only in W7-W8-W9.\n\nUnlikely.\n\nSo W9 is weak.\n\nSimilarly, W2,W3 \u2014 only in W2-W3-W4.\n\nW5,W6 \u2014 in W4-W5-W6 or W5-W6-W7.\n\nMore central.\n\nSo W5,W6 > W2,W3 > W9.\n\nBut I have five W tiles.\n\nPerhaps keep W5,W6,W9 for possible W4-W5-W6 and W7-W8-W9.\n\nBut need many draws.\n\nNot efficient.\n\nBack to Mixed Straight plan: T1-T2-T3, W4-W5-W6, T7-T8-T9.\n\nRequires T3, W4, T7.\n\nI have T1,T2,T8,T9,W5,W6 \u2014 good.\n\nT4 is not needed \u2014 so T4 can be discarded.\n\nT4 is not part of this core.\n\nIn fact, T4 might block the hand.\n\nBecause if I draw T3, I could make T1-T2-T3, but T4 is left.\n\nOr if I make T2-T3-T4, I lose T1.\n\nSo better to discard T4 to commit to T1-T2-T3.\n\nThen keep T1,T2,T8,T9,W5,W6.\n\nThen for pair, keep J2 (dragon).\n\nDiscard: B2, B5, F3, W2, W3, W9, T4\n\nFrom valid actions, I can discard T4.\n\nAlso B2, B5, F3, W2, W3, W9, T1, T2, etc.\n\nSo T4 is a valid action.\n\nIs T4 better to discard than F3?\n\nF3 is honor, no value.\n\nT4 is suited, could be used, but in this strategy, not.\n\nBut T4 has more potential than F3.\n\nFor example, if I shift to Bamboo chows, but I have no B3,B4,B6.\n\nNo.\n\nSo between T4 and F3, F3 is worse.\n\nBut let's see the answer choices: Play B2; Play T4; Play T9; Play W2; Play T1; Play W6; Play J2; Play F3; Play T2; Play W5; Play W3; Play B5; Play T8; Play W9\n\nI should not discard T1, T2, T8, T9, W5, W6 \u2014 they are core.\n\nSo eliminate Play T1, T2, T8, T9, W5, W6.\n\nAlso, J2 is potential dragon pair, so keep.\n\nB2, B5, F3, W2, W3, W9, T4 are candidates.\n\nAmong these, W9 is terminal, less useful.\n\nW2,W3: could be used, but W5,W6 are better.\n\nF3: no value.\n\nB2,B5: no support.\n\nT4: could be used in T2-T3-T4, but I have T2, so if I keep T2 and T4, I can wait for T3.\n\nBut I also have T1, so T1-T2-T3 is